[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch_server_access
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the routing control plane on the server access L3 switch. Specifically, FRRouting (ospfd) is not running on switch_server_access, which is the gateway/SVI for the 10.200.0.0/24 data center subnet.

Mechanism: With ospfd down, 10.200.0.0/24 is no longer advertised into the OSPF domain. The core and distribution routers thus lack a route to any 10.200.0.x address, so user subnets 10.1.1.0/24 and 10.2.1.0/24 cannot reach the data center. Inside the server VLAN, L2/bridge forwarding still works and the SVI (10.200.0.1) is local, so servers can ping each other and their gateway, matching the server team’s report.

Localization: On switch_server_access, systemctl shows frr.service “inactive (dead)” and “ospfd is not running.” Its routing table contains only connected routes: 10.200.0.0/24 (br0) and 172.16.0.10/31 (eth5). Router_core_3’s “show ip ospf neighbor” has no neighbor on the eth2/172.16.0.10/31 link to switch_server_access, confirming the adjacency is down. From core/dist devices, pings to 10.200.0.2 return “Network is unreachable,” and their OSPF tables do not contain 10.200.0.0/24.

Root cause: The FRR service on switch_server_access stopped, withdrawing 10.200.0.0/24 from OSPF and severing reachability to the data center from user pods.

Corrective fix: Restart FRR on switch_server_access and ensure it’s enabled to start at boot. Verify OSPF neighbors reestablish on eth5 (172.16.0.11/31 to router_core_3) and that 10.200.0.0/24 reappears in the core/distribution routing tables; then re-test reachability/DNS from hosts. Add monitoring/alerting for the FRR service and OSPF adjacencies. 
=== END ===